<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dhofar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dhofar is the southern governorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, with its capital at Salalah. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>-watered hills are the classic home of frankincense, traded since antiquity through ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumhuram">Sumhuram</a> (Khor Rori). Ruled at times by Hadramawt and by Yemeni dynasties such as the Rasulids, it passed to the sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> in the 19th century, and from 1963 to 1976 it was the scene of the Dhofar Rebellion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dhofar.jpg" alt="Dhofar" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NordNordWest · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dhofar_in_Oman_2016.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dhofar lies in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> and the Dhofar Mountains, a semi-circular range that runs into the sea at Ras Hamar and Mirbat. It has three distinct physical tracts: a cultivated coastal belt, Al Haffah, a desert plain about 60 km long, and the mountains. From mid-June to mid-September the south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>, known locally as Khareef, brings clouds that are trapped by the mountains, so that Dhofar is unique on the southern Arabian coast in having monsoon rainfall for about three months. The uplands rise to between 900 and 1,200 metres and slope away northwards towards the Empty Quarter. Frankincense grows wild in the dry northern wadis, where the dry air determines its quality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A site at Aybut Al-Auwal has yielded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> of the Arabian Nubian Complex, dated by two luminescence estimates to about 106,000 years ago, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> tomb complexes have been found at Hodor. Dhofar was a major exporter of frankincense in ancient times, some of it traded as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Al-Baleed, near Salalah, was home to the Manjawi civilisation from the 12th to the 16th centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>, writing about 1300, called Dufar a great and noble city whose people were Muslims, with a Count subject to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>. Before Omani rule it was part of the sultanate of Kathiri, and was later controlled mostly by the Al-Hakli (Qara) tribes.</p><p>In 1876 Sayyid Fadhl bin Alawi, who had come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, made himself de facto ruler and claimed allegiance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, but local sheikhs expelled him in 1879. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> administered Dhofar as a separate holding, and in the 1917 Gazetteer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> frankincense made up most of its trade. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> it supplied much of the food and grain for the British Army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. From 1963 the Omani Civil War was fought there between the Sultan's forces and the Dhofar Liberation Front and then the Popular Front for the Liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. The campaign was declared over in December 1975.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dhofar is the largest of the eleven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">governorates</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> by area, covering 99,300 square kilometres, and its capital and largest city is Salalah, which has an international airport and a major seaport. It has ten wilayats. The local Arabic dialect, Dhofari Arabic, differs from that of the rest of Oman, and many people speak Modern South Arabian languages such as Shehri, also called Jibbali, and Harsusi. The Al Kathiri are the largest tribe in Salalah and Dhofar. The region has long been known as a source of frankincense.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dhofar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Saba also lay on the caravan routes that carried frankincense and myrrh, gathered from trees in Hadramawt, <b>Dhofar</b> and the Horn of Africa, north through western Arabia to Gaza and the Mediterranean and to Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It provided bases and supplies to the guerrillas of the <b>Dhofar</b> rebellion against the Sultanate of Oman until the rebellion was defeated in the mid-1970s, and it backed the leftist National Democratic Front in North Yemen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-yemen">South Yemen · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 16th century Sultan Badr Abu Tuwayriq expanded Kathiri rule across Hadramawt and into <b>Dhofar</b>, using firearms and troops obtained through Ottoman contacts.</blockquote><a href="/state/kathiri-sultanate">Kathiri Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Its wealth came from frankincense, gathered in <b>Dhofar</b> where Hadramawt founded the port of Sumhuram (Khor Rori), and shipped through Qana on the Indian Ocean coast, a port described in the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hadramawt">Kingdom of Hadramawt</a></li><li><blockquote>Under al-Muzaffar Yusuf, who reigned for 45 years and took <b>Dhofar</b> in 1279, the state reached from Mecca's borders to Dhofar.</blockquote><a href="/state/rasulid-dynasty">Rasulid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Oil exports began in 1967 but development stayed slow while the <b>Dhofar</b> rebellion grew.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-muscat-and-oman">Sultanate of Muscat and Oman</a></li></ul></section>
